Order the certificate chain, and MoneyMoney's signature validates.

SecStaticCodeCheckValidity = 0. The application no longer says its own file seems to be damaged.

WHAT WAS WRONG. The CMS carries a SET of certificates with no order; producing an ORDERED chain,
leaf first and root last, is one of the things a real trust evaluation does, and the no-trustd
fallback was copying the set verbatim. "anchor" in the requirement language means the LAST entry of
the chain, so every requirement mentioning an anchor was asking whether MoneyMoney's own leaf was an
Apple certificate authority:

    anchor apple generic: chain has 4,
    anchor subject=Developer ID Application: MRH applications GmbH (9BE2AB75LL) isAppleCA=0

Now the chain is built by walking issuer to subject from the leaf:

    chain[0] = Developer ID Application: MRH applications GmbH (9BE2AB75LL)
    chain[1] = Developer ID Certification Authority
    chain[2] = Apple Root CA
    anchor apple generic: anchor subject=Apple Root CA isAppleCA=1

AND ONE MISTAKE OF MINE IN THE MIDDLE OF THAT, worth keeping because it looked so reasonable. The
first version appended any certificate that was not on the path, on the grounds that an odd
certificate set should not silently lose entries. MoneyMoney's set contains the leaf TWICE, so the
chain came out leaf, DevID CA, Apple Root, leaf -- with the duplicate leaf LAST, which is exactly the
entry anchor means, and the anchor test failed identically to before the ordering. A CHAIN IS A
PATH, NOT A SET. Certificates that are not on the path are not part of it.

THE VERDICT, walked down over two rungs as each cause was removed:

    -67061  a false label: an unverifiable timestamp discarding a good signature   security/0007
    -50     the trust evaluation never happened                                    security/0008
    100022  errSecErrnoBase + EINVAL from fcntl(F_NOCACHE)                         xnu/0020
    -67055  NO requirement could be parsed: the parser was never reachable         security/0009
    -67021  two nested components failed a requirement that could not match
    0       valid

MoneyMoney now gets past its own code signature check and reaches "Opening database". It stops there
on a different alert -- it cannot delete its temporary database file under
/Users/root/Library/Application Support -- which is a filesystem question and a fresh piece of work.

The evaluator trace that found this is kept: it prints the whole chain in order beside the anchor
verdict, because "the anchor is wrong" and "the chain is unordered" look identical when only the last
entry is printed.
--- a/OSX/sec/Security/SecTrust.c
+++ b/OSX/sec/Security/SecTrust.c
@@ -1703,7 +1703,61 @@
                     bool cider_lenient = (cider_strict == NULL || cider_strict[0] == '\0');
 
                     if (cider_lenient) {
-                        trust->_chain = CFArrayCreateCopy(NULL, trust->_certificates);
+                        /*
+                         * ORDERED LEAF FIRST, ROOT LAST, by issuer/subject linkage.
+                         *
+                         * The CMS carries a SET of certificates with no order; producing an ordered
+                         * chain is one of the things a real trust evaluation does. Copying the set
+                         * verbatim left MoneyMoney's leaf as the last entry, and "anchor" means the
+                         * LAST entry -- so every requirement mentioning an anchor was asking "is the
+                         * leaf an Apple CA?" and being told no:
+                         *
+                         *   anchor apple generic: chain has 4,
+                         *   anchor subject=Developer ID Application: MRH applications GmbH (9BE2AB75LL)
+                         *   isAppleCA=0
+                         *
+                         * Index 0 is the leaf by definition here (SecTrustGetCertificateAtIndex
+                         * returns _certificates[0] for index 0), so walk issuers from it.
+                         *
+                         * ANYTHING NOT ON THAT PATH IS DROPPED, and the first version of this got
+                         * that wrong. It appended the unlinked leftovers "so an odd certificate set
+                         * does not silently lose entries", and MoneyMoney's set contains the leaf
+                         * TWICE, so the chain came out [leaf, DevID CA, Apple Root, leaf] -- with the
+                         * duplicate leaf last, which is precisely the entry "anchor" means. The
+                         * anchor test then asked whether the leaf was an Apple CA and was told no,
+                         * exactly as before the ordering. A chain is a PATH, not a set.
+                         */
+                        CFIndex certN = CFArrayGetCount(trust->_certificates);
+                        CFMutableArrayRef ordered = CFArrayCreateMutable(NULL, certN, &kCFTypeArrayCallBacks);
+                        CFMutableSetRef placed = CFSetCreateMutable(NULL, 0, NULL);
+                        SecCertificateRef walk = (SecCertificateRef)CFArrayGetValueAtIndex(trust->_certificates, 0);
+
+                        while (walk != NULL) {
+                            CFArrayAppendValue(ordered, walk);
+                            CFSetAddValue(placed, walk);
+
+                            CFDataRef issuer = SecCertificateCopyIssuerSequence(walk);
+                            SecCertificateRef next = NULL;
+
+                            if (issuer) {
+                                for (CFIndex ix = 0; ix < certN; ix++) {
+                                    SecCertificateRef cand = (SecCertificateRef)CFArrayGetValueAtIndex(trust->_certificates, ix);
+                                    if (CFSetContainsValue(placed, cand))
+                                        continue;   /* also stops a self-signed root looping */
+                                    CFDataRef subject = SecCertificateCopySubjectSequence(cand);
+                                    bool match = subject && CFEqual(subject, issuer);
+                                    CFReleaseNull(subject);
+                                    if (match) {
+                                        next = cand;
+                                        break;
+                                    }
+                                }
+                                CFRelease(issuer);
+                            }
+                            walk = next;
+                        }
+                        CFRelease(placed);
+                        trust->_chain = ordered;
                     } else {
                         SecCertificateRef leafCert = (SecCertificateRef)CFArrayGetValueAtIndex(trust->_certificates, 0);
                         trust->_chain = CFArrayCreate(NULL, (const void**)&leafCert, 1, &kCFTypeArrayCallBacks);
--- a/OSX/libsecurity_codesigning/lib/reqinterp.cpp
+++ b/OSX/libsecurity_codesigning/lib/reqinterp.cpp
@@ -26,6 +26,8 @@
